 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, the worker evicts entries until the new sheet fits. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction
+
+The cache uses a two-queue (2Q) eviction policy. Two structures hold live entries:
+
+- **Q1** — a FIFO queue for newly admitted sheets. Every completed sheet enters Q1 on admission.
+- **Qm** — an LRU queue for sheets referenced more than once. A Q1 hit promotes the entry to Qm's head; a Qm hit moves it to Qm's head. Promotion is a move: total cache byte usage is unchanged.
+
+**Pre-admission eviction**: A single pass runs before each admission, evicting from Q1's tail until the new sheet fits within both the Q1 byte budget and the 12 GiB total budget. If Q1 drains completely and space is still needed, the pass continues evicting from Qm's tail.
+
+**Q1 byte budget**: Q1 is limited to a configurable fraction of the 12 GiB budget (default: 20%, ~2.4 GiB), set at startup. The Q1 budget must be at least one maximum sheet size; setting it smaller is a misconfiguration.
+
+During a sequential screening, newly admitted sheets cycle through Q1. Once Q1 reaches its byte budget, each new admission evicts the oldest Q1 entry — scan sheets do not reach Qm and do not displace comparison sheets already promoted by repeated access.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (FIFO Q1 + LRU Qm) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Editors alternate sequential screenings with repeated comparison sessions. LRU exposes comparison sheets to scan eviction. Frequency-based eviction with aging protects popular clips but requires counter maintenance and aging, and can retain stale favorites after a project switch. 2Q shields promoted sheets from scan churn with no counter bookkeeping, at the cost of a tunable Q1 byte budget. |
+| Q1 default byte budget | 20% of cache budget (~2.4 GiB) | Higher (larger scan buffer); lower (more Qm headroom) | At 2 MiB per sheet, 20% absorbs ~1,200 unique screening sheets before Q1 begins recycling. The remaining ~9.6 GiB holds ~4,800 Qm sheets. Tunable at startup. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. **Admission serialization**: Up to four render jobs may complete simultaneously and attempt concurrent admission. The required serialization or lock scope is not specified; this applies to the existing PREVIEW-004 eviction requirement regardless of eviction policy.
+2. **Concurrent promotion and eviction on the same entry**: Whether a Q1 hit and a tail-eviction targeting the same entry resolve cleanly under concurrent access is unspecified; this is subsumed by the admission serialization question above.
